    </div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;"><div class="container prose" style="max-width:42rem;">
    <p>You do not need to have it all figured out before you reach out. Diego can be your first conversation: a calm place to lay out the situation, understand whether a short sale is worth exploring, and leave with a clear next step. A short sale is not just a listing. It is a sequence of decisions, paperwork, and lender communication, and most homeowners have never done one before. From that first conversation to closing day, you and Diego will work through each stage together, Diego will recommend the next step along the way, and together you will bring in an attorney, CPA, lender, or housing counselor whenever a question belongs with them.</p>

    <h2>What a short sale is (in plain English)</h2>
    <p>A short sale happens when you sell your home for less than the amount you still owe on the mortgage, and your lender agrees to accept that lower amount as part of the sale.</p>
    <p>The key word is <em>agrees</em>. In a normal sale, the loan is paid off at closing and you keep any money left over. In a short sale, the sale does not bring in enough to pay the loan in full, so the lender (and sometimes more than one lender, if there is a second mortgage or home equity line) has to review the situation and approve the terms before the sale can close.</p>
    <p>A few important points:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The lender decides.</strong> A strong, well-documented request helps, but approval is up to the lender. No one can guarantee it.</li>
      <li><strong>It is still a real sale.</strong> The home is listed, shown, and sold to a buyer, with an added stage of lender review.</li>
      <li><strong>What happens to the remaining balance varies.</strong> Some lenders forgive the difference; others may keep the right to collect it, often called a "deficiency." That depends on your loan documents, your lender's terms, and Texas law, and it is a question for a real estate attorney.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who a short sale may fit</h2>
    <p>It may be worth exploring if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You owe more on your home than it would likely sell for today, after typical closing costs.</li>
      <li>You are facing a real, documented hardship, such as a job loss, reduced income, divorce, illness, a death in the family, or a required relocation.</li>
      <li>You can no longer afford the home over the long term and want a structured way forward.</li>
      <li>You would rather sell on a planned timeline than wait to see what happens.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who it may not fit</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You have equity.</strong> If your home is worth more than you owe, a traditional sale is usually simpler and lets you keep the proceeds.</li>
      <li><strong>Your hardship is temporary.</strong> Your servicer may offer a repayment plan, forbearance, or loan modification. A HUD-approved housing counselor can help you understand those options.</li>
      <li><strong>You want to keep the home.</strong> A short sale means selling. If staying is the goal, your servicer and a housing counselor are key conversations.</li>
      <li><strong>The tax or legal side is unclear.</strong> Forgiven debt can sometimes have tax consequences, and deficiency rules depend on your situation. Speak with a CPA and a real estate attorney before you commit.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Not sure which group you are in? That is exactly what the first conversation with Diego is for.</p>

    <h2>The process, step by step, with Diego alongside you</h2>
    <p>Every lender and every file is different, so treat this as a general map, not a schedule. What stays the same is the approach: at each stage, you and Diego will look at where things stand together, Diego will recommend the next step, and you will know who should answer the questions that fall outside real estate.</p>

    <h3>1. Start with a first conversation</h3>
    <p><strong>What you and Diego do:</strong> You and Diego talk through what you owe, what the home might sell for, your timeline, and what matters most to your family. Before anything is listed, Diego will run a comparable-based estimate of value so you can both see whether you likely have equity or are truly "underwater."</p>
    <p><strong>Diego's next-step recommendation:</strong> If a short sale looks like a reasonable path, Diego will lay out what comes next. If the numbers point to a traditional sale or a conversation with your servicer instead, he will tell you that honestly.</p>
    <p><strong>Your next action:</strong> Gather your most recent mortgage statement(s), including any second loan, and any letters you have received from your lender.</p>

    <h3>2. Bring in the right specialists early</h3>
    <p><strong>What you and Diego do:</strong> Together you identify the legal and tax questions in your situation, since some of the most important ones in a short sale are not real estate questions. Diego will help you figure out which kind of professional you need and what to ask, so those conversations start with the right facts.</p>
    <p><strong>Diego's next-step recommendation:</strong> Diego will recommend talking with a real estate attorney about deficiency and any agreements you may be asked to sign, a CPA about possible tax effects of forgiven debt, and your servicer about your account status. If you want free, neutral guidance, a HUD-approved housing counselor is a good call.</p>
    <p><strong>Your next action:</strong> Schedule those conversations and share what you learn with Diego so the plan reflects their guidance.</p>

    <h3>3. Set a price the lender can accept</h3>
    <p><strong>What you and Diego do:</strong> You and Diego review recent sales, active competition, and the home's condition, and set a price grounded in the market rather than in what is owed. Lenders usually order their own valuation, so this matters.</p>
    <p><strong>Diego's next-step recommendation:</strong> Diego will recommend which small, practical repairs or prep steps are worth doing and which ones are not worth your limited time and money.</p>
    <p><strong>Your next action:</strong> Agree on the price and a short prep list together.</p>

    <h3>4. Build a complete lender package</h3>
    <p><strong>What you and Diego do:</strong> You and Diego work through the documents lenders typically request: a hardship letter, proof of income, bank statements, tax returns, and a financial worksheet. Diego will explain what each one is for and help you keep the package complete and consistent.</p>
    <p><strong>Diego's next-step recommendation:</strong> Diego will recommend organizing the package before or at listing, not after an offer arrives, and will flag gaps before they cause delays. Your attorney or housing counselor can review anything you are unsure about signing.</p>
    <p><strong>Your next action:</strong> Work through the checklist one item at a time; you do not have to do it all in one day.</p>

    <h3>5. List, market, and choose the right offer</h3>
    <p><strong>What you and Diego do:</strong> Diego lists and markets the home, handles showings, and shares buyer feedback with you, with clear disclosure that the sale is subject to lender approval. When offers arrive, you and Diego review them side by side.</p>
    <p><strong>Diego's next-step recommendation:</strong> Diego will recommend the offer most likely to hold up through a long lender review, not only the highest one. Buyer patience and financing strength matter in a short sale.</p>
    <p><strong>Your next action:</strong> Decide together, and sign only after any questions for your attorney are answered.</p>

    <h3>6. Stay steady through lender review</h3>
    <p><strong>What you and Diego do:</strong> This is often the longest stage. The lender may approve, counter, ask for more paperwork, or decline. Diego follows up with the lender's short-sale department and keeps the buyer's side informed, and you and Diego go over each lender response together in plain language.</p>
    <p><strong>Diego's next-step recommendation:</strong> For every response, Diego will recommend how to answer it. If a foreclosure date has been scheduled, he will recommend confirming with your attorney and servicer right away, because a short sale does not automatically pause foreclosure.</p>
    <p><strong>Your next action:</strong> Respond quickly to document requests and keep your attorney in the loop on any lender counteroffer.</p>

    <h3>7. Close with your eyes open</h3>
    <p><strong>What you and Diego do:</strong> If the lender approves, you and Diego move toward closing with a title company. Diego coordinates the timeline with title, the buyer's agent, and the lender, and helps you plan the move.</p>
    <p><strong>Diego's next-step recommendation:</strong> Diego will recommend having your attorney review the approval letter, especially anything about the remaining balance, before you sign.</p>
    <p><strong>Your next action:</strong> Confirm move-out timing and keep copies of every closing document for your CPA.</p>

    <h2>Who does what</h2>
    <p><strong>Diego, as your REALTOR® and first point of contact,</strong> is with you from the first conversation through closing. He leads the real estate side and keeps the whole process organized: pricing, preparation, listing and marketing, the lender package, lender and buyer communication, negotiation, and closing coordination, with a next-step recommendation at every stage, in English or Spanish.</p>
    <p><strong>An attorney</strong> answers deficiency questions, reviews the approval letter and agreements, and handles bankruptcy, foreclosure defense, or other legal strategies.</p>
    <p><strong>A CPA or tax professional</strong> answers whether forgiven debt could be treated as income and how a short sale may affect your taxes.</p>
    <p><strong>Your lender or a HUD-approved housing counselor</strong> explains your account status, credit reporting questions, and alternatives such as modification, forbearance, or a deed in lieu of foreclosure.</p>

    <h2>Common myths and mistakes</h2>
    <p><strong>"If I list as a short sale, the lender has to accept."</strong> Lenders are not required to approve. A well-prepared file helps, but approval is never guaranteed.</p>
    <p><strong>"A short sale stops foreclosure."</strong> Not by itself. The two can run at the same time. If you have received foreclosure notices, speak with an attorney and your servicer right away.</p>
    <p><strong>"Once it closes, I don't owe anything."</strong> That depends on your lender's approval terms. Have an attorney review the approval.</p>
    <p><strong>"I'll deal with it later."</strong> Waiting often narrows your options. Starting early usually gives you more room to choose.</p>
    <p><strong>"I have to figure this out alone."</strong> You should not have to. Working through it step by step with someone alongside you is less stressful than trying to piece it together yourself.</p>

    <h2>How working with Diego looks</h2>
    <p>Diego works the same calm, structured way with every seller: real numbers first, honest options, and a clear next step. In a short sale, that means you are never left wondering what happens now.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>First conversation.</strong> A private, no-pressure call about your home, your loan, your timeline, and your goals. You and Diego get an honest read on whether a short sale fits, and Diego will recommend what to do first.</li>
      <li><strong>Options and a plan.</strong> If a short sale makes sense, you and Diego map the stages above with your specific next actions, and Diego will help you connect with an attorney, CPA, or housing counselor for the questions that belong with them. If another path fits better, he will say so.</li>
      <li><strong>Together through closing.</strong> From pricing and paperwork to lender follow-up and closing day, you and Diego move through each step together, with plain-language updates and a next-step recommendation at every decision point.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Diego is originally from Argentina and works in both English and Spanish, so your family can ask real questions without translation friction.</p>

